---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Rostr from "../components/Rostr.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How rostr works"
  description="How rostr drafts a name for each Claude Code and Codex session, titles it, and frees the name again."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>How <Rostr /> works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Claude Code and Codex session gets a name from your roster and a short summary of what it's doing, like <span
        class="session-title">🧹 Ginny · Fix flaky login test</span>. Here's how that works under the hood.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      <code>rostr assemble</code> installs hooks into Claude Code and Codex. Each hook calls the <Rostr /> CLI, which
      keeps your rosters and who's drafted where: in a local SQLite database, or on the <Rostr /> API in cloud mode, so
      sessions on every machine draft from the same rosters.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a session's first prompt, <Rostr /> drafts a name, tells the agent who it is, and puts the name in the session's
      title. While the session works, the hooks keep the name alive. When it ends, the name goes back to the bench.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Drafting a name</h2>
    <p>Each session is drafted from the roster linked to its project, else your default roster. The pick goes:</p>
    <ol class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Least recently drafted.</strong> The free member drafted longest ago, so names cycle through the whole
        roster.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Take a resting name.</strong> If everyone's busy, the name of the session that has rested longest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Copies.</strong> If no one is resting either, a numbered copy like 🧙 Gandalf 2. A session always gets a
        name.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>A resumed session gets its old name back, if no one else has taken it since.</p>
    <p>
      In cloud mode, Claude Code sessions draft in the background as soon as they start, so the first prompt doesn't
      wait on the network.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Resting and benching</h2>
    <p>
      A member is <strong>drafted</strong>, <strong>resting</strong>, or on the <strong>bench</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Resting.</strong> A session that finished its turn and gets no new prompt for 15 minutes is resting. It
        keeps its name, but when every other name is taken, a new session can take it. If that happens, the resting
        session gets a new name on its next prompt, and the agent is told.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep-alive.</strong> Each prompt and turn tells <Rostr /> the session is still around, and later prompts
        check in at most once an hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Benching.</strong> When a session ends, its name goes back to the bench. A session that's never seen
        again, like a closed desktop session, is benched after 7 days (<code>rostr config expiry</code>), and{" "}
        <code>rostr bench &lt;name&gt;</code> frees a name sooner.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Telling the agent who it is</h2>
    <p>
      The hook tells the agent its name, and to sign anything it writes outside the chat with it, like PR descriptions
      and Slack messages. After compaction, it's told again.
    </p>
    <p>
      Commands the agent runs, like <code>rostr task</code>, need to know which session they're in. In Claude Code, the
      start hook appends <code>export ROSTR_SESSION=&lt;session ID&gt;</code> to the session's env file (the path Claude
      Code passes in <code>CLAUDE_ENV_FILE</code>), and Claude Code loads that file before every shell command the agent
      runs. Codex sets <code>CODEX_THREAD_ID</code> in its commands' environment itself, so <Rostr /> reads that. The
      <code>rostr task</code> command the agent is told to run also names the session with <code>--session</code>, so
      it works even where neither variable is set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The hooks</h2>
    <p>
      Both agents get the same five hooks. Each one is a one-liner that runs <code>rostr hook</code> only if <Rostr /> is
      installed, so the logic lives in the binary and updates when you upgrade. After an upgrade, the first session
      reinstalls the hooks if they've changed.
    </p>
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>SessionStart</code></dt>
        <dd>
          Claude Code: records the session in <code>ROSTR_SESSION</code> and, in cloud mode, drafts ahead. Both: get the
          old name back on resume, and re-tell the name after compaction. Codex: drafts a forked thread.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>UserPromptSubmit</code></dt>
        <dd>
          First prompt: drafts, tells the agent who it is, and sets the title. Later prompts: end a rest, keep the name
          alive, and retitle the session if its task changed.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>PostToolUse</code></dt>
        <dd>
          Marks the session as working again. Claude Code: after a title call, records the summary as the task, and ends
          the turn if the answer is already written.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>Stop</code></dt>
        <dd>Records that the turn ended, which starts the 15 minutes until the session rests.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <dt><code>SessionEnd</code></dt>
        <dd>Benches the name and clears the pane label.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Hooks never block a session. Network calls time out after 3 seconds, and anything slow runs in the background. If{" "}
      <Rostr /> can't draft, the session just starts unnamed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Titles in Claude Code</h2>
    <p>
      The first-prompt hook sets the title to <span class="session-title">&lt;name&gt; · &lt;start of your prompt&gt;</span>,
      so the name shows straight away. Then a 3-5 word summary replaces the prompt, written by <Rostr /> or by the agent,
      depending on where the session runs:
    </p>
    <table class="surface-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where the session runs</th>
          <th>How the summary gets there</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Terminal</th>
          <td>
            <Rostr /> summarizes your first prompt in the background with <code>claude -p --model haiku</code>, on your own
            login, and puts the summary in the terminal title on your next prompt
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Desktop app, Local session</th>
          <td>
            The agent writes its answer, then calls the app's <code>set_session_title</code> at the end of the same
            message. <Rostr />'s PostToolUse hook ends the turn there, so the answer stays the last thing on screen
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Desktop app via Remote Control, and cloud sessions</th>
          <td>
            The agent calls Claude Code's remote <code>set_session_title</code> first, before writing its answer
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The summary is recorded as the session's task, which <code>rostr stats</code> shows. When the task changes, later
      prompts retitle the session to match.
    </p>
    <h3>Why the agent writes the summary in the app</h3>
    <p>
      Hooks can only set a title when a prompt arrives. In a terminal, that's the best there is: the summary shows up
      on your second prompt. The app can do better, because it gives the agent a title tool. The agent already knows
      the task, so it titles the session during its first reply.
    </p>
    <p>
      The title tool is also the only option in Remote Control and cloud sessions, where a hook's title doesn't show up
      at all (a <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/104473">known Claude Code bug</a>).
    </p>
    <h3>When in the reply the title gets set</h3>
    <p>
      After any tool call, Claude normally writes another message, so a title call could leave an extra message after
      your answer. To avoid that:
    </p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Local sessions:</strong> the agent answers first and sets the title last. <Rostr />'s PostToolUse hook
        then ends the turn, so your answer stays the last message.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remote Control and cloud sessions:</strong> the agent sets the title first, then answers. Those apps
        show text written just before a tool call as a progress update, so an answer followed by a title call would show
        up once as an update, then again in full.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Titles in Codex</h2>
    <p>
      The first-prompt hook drafts a name and names the thread <span class="session-title"
        >&lt;name&gt; · &lt;start of your prompt&gt;</span> itself, through the Codex app-server, before the agent's
      first reply. Codex's terminal title shows the thread name, and a forked thread gets a name of its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Token use</h2>
    <p>
      <Rostr /> adds a few hundred tokens to a session, almost all on the first prompt. Later prompts add nothing unless
      the session is renamed.
    </p>
    <ul class="terse-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Every session.</strong> The <Rostr /> skill's one-line description sits in the agent's skill list, about 80
        tokens. The full skill loads only when you ask about <Rostr />.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>First prompt.</strong> The agent's identity, about 120 tokens. In the Claude Code desktop app and cloud
        sessions, the title request (about 100 more), and one short title call, which in Local sessions first loads the
        title tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Terminal sessions.</strong> No title call. Instead, <code>claude -p --model haiku</code> summarizes up to the
        first 4,000 characters of your first prompt, on your own login.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Now and then.</strong> After compaction, or when a resting session is renamed, the agent is told its
        name again, about 120 tokens.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>tmux and herdr</h2>
    <p>
      When a session is drafted or renamed, <Rostr /> labels its pane: herdr shows the name as the pane's agent, and in
      tmux a window with a single pane is renamed to it. The label is cleared when the session ends.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude Code runs its hooks inside the session's own pane, so <Rostr /> knows which pane to label. Codex runs hooks
      in a shared background process, whose pane is whichever terminal started it, and nothing ties a thread to the
      terminal showing it. So <Rostr /> works from the title instead: it has just named the thread, and Codex puts the
      thread's name in its terminal title. <Rostr /> labels the one Codex pane whose title shows the new name, and labels
      nothing if it finds none or more than one. If you set a custom <code>tui.terminal_title</code> in Codex, include{" "}
      <code>thread-name</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Privacy and security</h2>
    <p>
      Local mode makes no network calls. In cloud mode, tasks and repo names are encrypted on your machine before
      they're sent, and the server never sees your <Rostr /> key, prompts, or code.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-link"><a href="/privacy">Learn more about privacy →</a></p>
    <p class="docs-link"><a href="/cli">Read the full CLI docs →</a></p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    padding: 72px 0 40px;
  }

  .page-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6vw, 52px);
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }

  .section p {
    max-width: 40em;
  }

  .rows {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 10px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .row dt code {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .row dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 4px;
    }
  }

  .surface-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .surface-table th,
  .surface-table td {
    padding: 10px 8px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .surface-table thead th {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .surface-table tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .surface-table td {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .terse-list {
    margin: 16px 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
    max-width: 44em;
  }

  .terse-list li {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
</style>
